+---
+id: task-002-fix-order-service-bugs-coupon-discount-base-coupon-expiry-boundary-coupon-giveback-on-cancel
+type: task
+title: "Fix order-service bugs: coupon discount base, coupon expiry boundary, coupon giveback on cancel"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Three reports this week:
+
+1. Finance: orders that combine a volume discount with a percentage coupon get more off than they
+   should. Twelve notebooks at €5.00 each with a 10% coupon come to €48.00 of goods; expected €48.60.
+2. Luca (customer): the code AUTUMN was advertised as valid until 30 November. He ordered on
+   30 November and the code was refused as expired.
+3. Anna (customer): her receipt shows VAT on the delivery cost, €1.08 on €4.90 of shipping. She asks
+   that VAT stop being added to delivery, since it is a service, not goods.
+
+## Root causes and fixes
+
+1. `priceOrder` (`src/pricing.ts`) computed the coupon's discount with `couponDiscount(coupon,
+   grossTotal)`, i.e. on the lines' gross amount. The README states a coupon "applies to the order's
+   subtotal after volume discounts". Fixed by computing the coupon on `afterVolumeTotal` (gross minus
+   the volume discount) instead of on the gross total. The 50%-of-gross cap (`capped`) is unchanged,
+   since that cap is explicitly on the gross amount.
+2. `Coupons.redeemable` (`src/coupons.ts`) rejected a coupon as expired when `date` was on or after
+   `validUntil` (`>= 0`). The README states a coupon is "valid from its first day to its last day,
+   both included". Changed the comparison to `> 0`, so the last day itself is still valid.
+3. Not a bug: the README is explicit that "Shipping is subject to VAT at the standard rate (22%)",
+   and `test/orders.test.ts` already asserted `shippingVatCents: 108` on €4.90 of shipping before this
+   change — exactly the amount Anna received. The service is computing VAT on shipping as documented
+   and intended. No code change. See the decision-log for this determination
+   (`dl-001-keep-vat-on-shipping-anna-s-report-is-not-a-bug`) for the reasoning, in case the
+   business wants to change the rule itself later.
+
+While investigating, also found and fixed a fourth, unreported bug in the same area: `OrderService.cancel`
+(`src/orders.ts`) called `coupons.giveBack(order.coupon, order.customer)`, but `coupons.use` records the
+order's *id*, not its customer. A single-use coupon was therefore never actually given back on
+cancellation, contradicting the README ("Cancelling it returns its units and gives back its coupon, if
+the coupon was single-use"). Fixed by passing `order.id` instead of `order.customer`.
+
+## Tests
+
+Added one red-first regression test per real bug (red before the fix, green after), in
+`test/coupons.test.ts`:
+
+- "applies the coupon to the subtotal after the volume discount, not before" — 12 units at €2000 with
+  a 10% coupon: volume discount €24.00, coupon €21.60 (10% of €216.00 after volume discount), goods
+  €194.40.
+- "accepts a coupon on its last valid day, inclusive".
+- "gives a single-use coupon back when the order that used it is cancelled".
+
+No test added for report 3: it is not a bug, and the existing test
+(`test/orders.test.ts`, "prices a small order…") already covers and pins the current, correct VAT-on-
+shipping behaviour.
+
+All 20 tests in the suite pass (`npm test`).
+
+## Files changed
+
+- `src/pricing.ts`, `src/coupons.ts`, `src/orders.ts` (fixes)
+- `test/coupons.test.ts` (tests)
